  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite imagery reveals increasing cloud cover \nbeing lifted into the Upper Great Lakes as an upper trough works \nthrough the region. At the surface a low pressure center is swinging \nacross the Hudson Bay, dragging a surface cold front into the far \nwestern UP as of writing this discussion. Some light radar returns \nare noted on KMQT and KDLH from IWD down through northern WI along \nthe boundary. As this frontal boundary continues to press across the \nUP this morning, we should see temps finally come down into the 40s \nto low 50s by sunrise. Light showers will accompany the front across \nthe area today, bringing light rainfall amounts generally 0.1\" or \nless. Dry air filtering in behind the the front will work to lower \nafternoon RHs in the western UP between 25-45%, driest in Gogebic \nand Ontonagon counties, posing a limited fire weather risk. Luckily, \nwinds remain light (15 kts or less) and daytime temps only peak in \nthe 50s to near 60. Elsewhere, low 50s expected in the Keweenaw \nwhere breezy winds will be common and near 60 to low 60s across the \ns-central. Lows fall into the 30s and 40s, coldest interior.\n\nRidging begins to recover Monday into Tuesday, though an embedded \nshortwave lifts north over the Dakotas Monday night through Tuesday, \nsupporting a weak low to the west which may graze the western UP \nwith some rain showers Monday night through Tuesday afternoon. This \nquickly is followed up by a more vigorous shortwave moving over the \nsame area, sending another sfc low to the west of the CWA, keeping \nshower chances in the forecast through Wednesday night. \nDiscrepancies remain on exact track, but best rain chances (40-70%) \nare over the west during both events. Guidance quickly begins to \nstruggle after Thursday, but a more active period is anticipated to \ncontinue into early November captured by the CPC's 6-10 \nprecipitation outlook of slightly above normal precip. At this time, \nensembles don't suggest any widespread meaningful rain across the \narea with 24hr prob of QPF >0.1\" only reaching 60% on any given day. \nAdditionally, above normal temperatures dominate through the rest of \nthe month into early November with seemingly no end in sight. All \nglobal ensembles highlight strong positive 850mb temp anomalies even \ninto their extended 200+ hour range.",
